  "_note": "Channel catfish (Ictalurus punctatus), a rare whiskered bottom feeder of Midwest lakes and rivers. Shape (v4, checked against the real fish from the side, above and 3/4): a broad, flat head about a quarter of the length, wider than it is deep, with a blunt snout rounded from above and the wide mouth a little under it (the upper jaw longer); small eyes high on the head; a thick, round body, deepest ahead of the middle, flattening toward the head and pressed thin toward the tail; a short dorsal fin set forward with a stout spine; a small fleshy adipose fin; a long, round-edged anal fin; pelvic fins under the middle; spined pectorals low behind the head; a deeply forked tail with two about equal, rounded lobes. Markings from field-guide descriptions: no scales; eight barbels (\"whiskers\"), the two at the corners of the mouth longest; slate grey-blue above, paler blue-grey sides with scattered small dark spots, a white belly.",
